
----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTALLATION VIA COMPILATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

See also: http://pymolwiki.org/index.php/Linux_Install

REQUIREMENTS

    - C++17 compiler (e.g. gcc 8+)
    - CMake (3.13+)
    - Python 3.11+
    - pip (with build submodule <pip install build>; conda's package name is python-build)
    - OpenGL
    - GLEW
    - libpng
    - freetype
    - libxml2 (optional, for COLLADA export, disable with --libxml=no)
    - msgpack-c 2.1.5+ (optional, for fast MMTF loading and export,
        disable with --use-msgpackc=no)
    - mmtf-cpp (for fast MMTF export, disable with --use-msgpackc=no)
    - PySide6 (Qt GUI)
    - glm
    - catch2 (optional, enable with --testing=true)
    - openvr 1.0.x (optional, enable with --openvr=true)
    - libnetcdf (optional, disable with --vmd-plugins=no)
    - nlohmann-json 3.10+ (optional, for glTF 2.0 and GLB export,
        enable with --json=true)

SETUP OPTIONS

    pip install --help

    Special install options:
      --pymol-path=       installation directory for PyMOL data ($PYMOL_PATH)
      --no-launcher       skip installation of the pymol launcher

    Environment variables:
      PREFIX_PATH   Colon-delimited list of paths to search for headers and
                    libraries, e.g. $HOME/mmtf-cpp:$HOME/msgpack-c:/opt/local
      CXX           C++ compiler command
      CC            C compiler command
      CXXFLAGS      C++ compiler flags
      CFLAGS        C compiler and linker flags
      CPPFLAGS      C/C++ preprocessor flags, e.g. -I/tmp/msgpack-c/include
      LDFLAGS       linker flags

INSTALLATION

    Clean build and installation
      pip install .

    Recommended for developers (build output, incremental build, and building C++ unit tests)
      pip install --verbose --no-build-isolation --config-settings testing=True .

DEVELOPMENT LOOP

    Editable install: Python edits under modules/ take effect on the next
    launch without a rebuild; C++ and shader edits need the install command
    again (CMake rebuilds only what changed in build/).

      pip install --no-build-isolation --config-settings testing=True -e '.[dev]'

    Limit build parallelism with --config-settings jobs=N or JOBS=N.
    ccache: export CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_LAUNCHER=ccache before installing
    (CMake 3.17+ honors it; nothing in setup.py is needed).

    Tests (headless, skips ~/.pymolrc):
      pymol -ckqy testing/testing.py --run all

    Performance baseline (see docs/baseline.md):
      pymol -ckqy testing/bench.py

STANDALONE BUNDLE (no Python needed on the target machine)

    From an environment with pymol and the dev extras installed:

      pyinstaller --noconfirm packaging/pymolish.spec

    Produces dist/pymolish/ (one-folder). Run dist/pymolish/pymolish from
    anywhere; -c works headless. CI uploads this folder and the wheel as
    artifacts for Linux, Windows and macOS on every push (Python 3.12 job).

RUNNING PyMOL

    $PYMOL_PATH/bin/pymol
    or
    $PYTHONUSERBASE/bin/pymol

Good luck!
